failure_location.quote:
Introduce a function $F : [0, \infty) \to Y$ defined by 
    \begin{displaymath}
        F(t) := \frac{f(x + tv)}{\left\| v \right\|_X}
    \end{displaymath}
    for $t \geq 0$.

Then, the hypotheses
    \begin{enumerate}[label=\rm{(H\arabic*)}, ref=\rm{(H\arabic*)}, itemsep=1ex]
    \item $\partial^+_v f(x)$ and $\partial^-_v f(x)$ exist as extended real numbers, \label{H1}
    \item neither $\partial_v^{+} f(x)=\partial^-_v f(x)= \infty$ nor $\partial_v^{+} f(x)=\partial^-_v f(x)= - \infty$ \label{H2}
    \end{enumerate}
    hold if and only if the specular directional derivative $\partial^{\sd}_v f(x)$ exists

Then, it is well-known that, if $x \neq 0$, then $f$ is Fr\'echet differentiable at $x$ with 
    \begin{displaymath}
        Df(x)= \frac{x}{\left\| x \right\|_X}.
    \end{displaymath}
